{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0fafc4599cbdd5fecf29bc064c6b973660752fde216138551d5b41ade645fd17","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.05},{"probability":0.5,"value":2},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.05}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"WEIGHTS FIRST, because Wave 1 disagreed with itself and the lock settles it. The accounting basis is CONSTANT 2025 USD PPP, which FIXES the weights at 2025 shares: OECD-38 ~$75T, China ~$40T, so China ~0.34 and OECD ~0.66 for the whole window. Panelist 1 used 0.37 on the grounds that \"China's share is rising\" — that is a chain-weighted construction and the lock forbids it; it shows an anchoring slip toward the familiar market-share framing. P2's 0.32 and P3's 0.34 are closer; I use 0.34.\nTHE ATTACK ON WAVE 1'S SHARED BASELINE. All three took 2A's \"0.6%/yr OECD real GDP per capita\" as a forecast baseline and then added a small AI offset (P1 +0.4-0.6, P2 +0.30 less 0.15 digestion, P3 +0.30). 2A's own words are \"absent productivity offsets\" — it is a COUNTERFACTUAL constructed to isolate the aging drag, not a conditional forecast, and all three anchored on the locked prior's framing in exactly the way my brief names. What the 0.6% figure holds out is not only AI TFP. It holds out CAPITAL DEEPENING, which in a shrinking-workforce economy is close to arithmetic: labour input falls ~0.3%/yr in the OECD while the capital stock does not fall, so capital per worker rises and measured labour productivity rises with it at alpha ~0.35 — roughly +0.10-0.20pp/yr with no TFP contribution at all. And the deepening is not incidental: a labour-scarce economy with a care and construction wage signal is the strongest automation business case in the historical record, which is why all three Wave-1 coupling notes conclude AI-Q2 (adoption) should be revised UP. Adoption is the cheap, software-priced, wage-driven decision; it is barely touched by my high-hurdle-rate thesis. So OECD-38 = 0.6 per capita + 0.25 population (migration-driven; the PHYSICAL labour-migration channel is pulled by aging, not blocked) + 0.45 realized offset from deepening plus diffusion = 1.30%/yr.\nAGAINST MYSELF, HONESTLY: my CQ4 is the highest real-yield number on this panel, and a 2032-36 average real yield of 2.45% is a genuine investment drag and a genuine debt-service claim on GDP. That is the main reason I stop at 1.30 on the OECD leg. The two forces — deepening pulled by labour scarcity, investment rationed by the cost of capital — are the same automation decision seen from the quantity and the price side, and they substantially offset.\nCHINA 2032-36 published print = 3.30%/yr. Labour -0.80pp; capital +1.75pp with deteriorating ICOR as the marginal project turns from housing to grid and datacentre; TFP +1.30pp (China is the world's sharpest automation demand-pull: worker/retiree 4.4->2.8 with a domestic wage signal); pension-subsidy crowding -0.45pp. P3's point that the published print is argued to overstate true growth by ~1-2pp (Nakamura-Steinsson-Liu, satellite-luminosity work) is correct and is UPWARD on the resolving measure at a 0.34 weight; the print floor is also policy-anchored near 3% in practice. I keep 3.30 rather than P2's 2.80.\nCOMBINED P50 = 0.66 x 1.30 + 0.34 x 3.30 = 0.858 + 1.122 = 1.98 -> 2.00%. Above all three Wave-1 panelists (1.75 / 1.85 / 1.95), driven entirely by refusing to treat a counterfactual as a baseline and by the capital-deepening arithmetic they omitted.\nDISPERSION, with a deliberately fatter RIGHT tail than Wave 1. P10 = 1.05: deep capex digestion inside the window (AI-Q4 at 66% makes 2032-36 the digestion years — P2's timing point is correct and important), repression binding, OECD ~0.4 and China ~1.9. P90 = 3.05: the fiscally-priced-equilibrium world described below, where high real rates are the price signal that FORCES automation deepening rather than blocking it — OECD ~2.2 with China at 4.2. Sigma ~0.78 with slight left skew. Wave 1's P90s (2.65 / 2.80 / 2.85) all sit below 2.9, which is the signature of three forecasters who priced the drag channel in detail and the pull channel in a sentence.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.520Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed's HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed's Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for the 2027 and 2028 vintages, with no upward revision to the 2024-26 history.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest direct refutation of my central claim that the filter is revising upward into a regime break. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.45% toward 1.95-2.05% (Wave-1 territory), my variance attribution flips back to premium-dominant, CQ5 falls ~5pp to roughly 25 as the MOVE level channel weakens, and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 15 at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months, in the ABSENCE of an announced yield-curve-control, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate from the Federal Reserve or Treasury.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Mean reversion down without a repression mechanism is exactly the move Wave 1 asserted and I said had no mechanism. CQ4 P50 down ~50bp to ~1.95% and P90 down to ~3.2; CQ6-D up ~6pp and CQ6-A down ~4pp, since cheap duration signals abundant saving. If it instead happens WITH an announced mandate, my repression wedge was too small (-30bp) and only CQ4 moves, not CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation aggregate for 2028-2030 prints below 2.2%/yr on the Turkiye-INCLUSIVE basis, or the OECD discontinues the Turkiye-inclusive headline aggregate in favour of the ex-Turkiye series as its reference measure.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Either observation kills the measurement argument that took my CQ2 to 34 (top of the Wave-1 range) and that suppressed CQ6-D. CQ2 falls to ~24, CQ6-C falls from 19 to ~14 and CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~15. This is the signpost most specific to me, because two of three Wave-1 panelists never priced Turkiye at all.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >=25% occurs and G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields fall by >=100bp from the equity peak date to the trough date — i.e. the bond hedge works emphatically, not marginally.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms Wave 1's disinflationary-bust premise against my credit-event argument and resolves CQ5's conjunct B as NO for the likeliest shock in the window. CQ5 falls from 30 to ~18. It also implies abundant precautionary saving, so CQ4 P50 falls ~35bp and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 16.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Japan's 10y JGB nominal yield remains below 2.25% AND Germany's 10y Bund below 3.00% on a twelve-month average, with the Bank of Japan holding a policy rate at or below 1.0%.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the Japan/Europe convergence-upward component worth ~40-50bp of my CQ4 recomposition and vindicates the low-weighted-average reading all three Wave-1 panelists reached (by a route panelist 2 described in words and then neutralized). CQ4 P50 down ~40bp to ~2.05%; CQ5 down ~4pp via the MOVE level channel; CQ3 and CQ6 essentially unchanged, since this is a price signpost, not a quantity one.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 redteam seat, effort 'rstar'."}}